    <TooltipProvider>
      <Tooltip tooltip="This is a tooltip!">
        <button>Click me!</button>
      </Tooltip>
      <Tooltip tooltip="This is another tooltip!">
        <button>Click me!</button>
      </Tooltip>
    </TooltipProvider>
  );
};

export default App;
```

In this example, we create a `TooltipContext` using `createContext` from React. We then create a `TooltipProvider` component that wraps the app and provides the `tooltip` state, `openTooltip` function, and `closeTooltip` function to all its children.

The `Tooltip` component uses the `useContext` hook to access the `closeTooltip` function from the context and renders the tooltip content only when the `tooltip` state is truthy. When the user clicks the "Close" button, the `closeTooltip` function is called, which sets the `tooltip` state to `null`, closing the tooltip.

This way, when the user clicks on a button that opens a tooltip, the previously opened tooltip is automatically closed. This is achieved by using React Context to share the state between components. 

